<?xml version="1.0" encoding="ISO-8859-1" ?>
<!DOCTYPE scenario SYSTEM "sipp.dtd">

<!--
  A customer's softphone logging in. Expects the login to SUCCEED.

  This is the exchange MicroSIP, Zoiper and PortSIP all perform on startup:
  an unauthenticated REGISTER, a 401 carrying a nonce, then the same REGISTER
  with a digest response computed from the password the customer was given.

  What it proves is narrow and important: the HA1 that provisioning derived
  from a generated password, wrote to a db_text file, and moved into place, is
  the same value a client computes from that password. Any disagreement along
  that chain reaches the customer as "Forbidden" with a correct password, and
  there is nothing in the switch logs that explains it.

  PARAMETERS ARE SPLIT ACROSS TWO MECHANISMS, AND NOT BY PREFERENCE.

    -au / -ap        the account being AUTHENTICATED as, and its password
    -inf field0      realm / SIP domain
    -inf field1      the account claimed in From
    -inf field2      the account claimed in To — the ADDRESS OF RECORD

  From and To are separate fields because OpenSIPS's registrar takes the AOR
  from the TO header (RFC 3261 §10.2), not From. A scenario that puts one value
  in both cannot exercise the takeover where a caller authenticates correctly,
  satisfies a From check, and registers a binding for somebody else — which is
  exactly the hole an earlier config had, and exactly why an earlier version of
  this file could not see it.

  The credentials cannot be injection fields: SIPp substitutes [fieldN] before
  it parses keywords, so [authentication username=[field1]] does not nest --
  it emits the field value, then the literal keyword text, with the username
  taken from uninitialised memory. It produces a message that looks almost
  right and authenticates as garbage.

  The two identities are separate parameters so this same exchange covers the
  forgery where a caller authenticates correctly and claims another account.
  See sipp-register-denied.xml, which is this scenario expecting a 403.
-->
<scenario name="MEGATRON register">

  <send retrans="500">
    <![CDATA[

      REGISTER sip:[field0] SIP/2.0
      Via: SIP/2.0/[transport] [local_ip]:[local_port];branch=[branch]
      Max-Forwards: 70
      From: <sip:[field1]@[field0]>;tag=[call_number]
      To: <sip:[field2]@[field0]>
      Call-ID: [call_id]
      CSeq: 1 REGISTER
      Contact: <sip:[field1]@[local_ip]:[local_port]>
      Expires: 300
      User-Agent: MEGATRON-lab
      Content-Length: 0

    ]]>
  </send>

  <!-- auth="true" keeps the challenge for the next request to answer. -->
  <recv response="401" auth="true"/>

  <send retrans="500">
    <![CDATA[

      REGISTER sip:[field0] SIP/2.0
      Via: SIP/2.0/[transport] [local_ip]:[local_port];branch=[branch]
      Max-Forwards: 70
      From: <sip:[field1]@[field0]>;tag=[call_number]
      To: <sip:[field2]@[field0]>
      Call-ID: [call_id]
      CSeq: 2 REGISTER
      Contact: <sip:[field1]@[local_ip]:[local_port]>
      Expires: 300
      [authentication]
      User-Agent: MEGATRON-lab
      Content-Length: 0

    ]]>
  </send>

  <recv response="200"/>

</scenario>
